  { q: 'What does the raven mean in Poe\'s poem?', a: 'In "The Philosophy of Composition" (1846), Poe himself wrote that the raven was chosen for its symbolic aptness — a bird of "ill omen" — and that "Nevermore" was chosen as the refrain because of its sonority and meaning. The raven in the poem does not predict the future; it repeats one word, and the narrator projects his grief onto it. Poe described the narrator as a man who "self-tortures himself" with the delusion that the bird has supernatural meaning.' },
