    <h1>Edgar Allan Poe's "The Raven"</h1>
    <p class="lore-subtitle">The Poem, the Bird, and the Context</p>
  </header>

  <section class="lore-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Edgar Allan Poe's "The Raven" was first published in <em>The American Review</em> in January 1845. It describes a grieving narrator visited by a raven that speaks only "Nevermore". In <em>The Philosophy of Composition</em> (1846), Poe explained his choices: the raven was selected as a symbol of "ill omen" and the refrain chosen for its sonority. The poem is fictional; the raven in it carries the meaning the narrator — explicitly described as self-torturing — projects onto it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Poem — Context and Key Facts</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Key facts about the poem and its publication:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>First published: <em>The American Review</em>, January 1845. Simultaneously reprinted in the <em>Evening Mirror</em>.</li>
      <li>Author: Edgar Allan Poe (1809–1849).</li>
      <li>Length: 108 lines, 18 six-line stanzas.</li>
      <li>Metre: Trochaic octameter — a heavily stressed, rhythmically insistent metre that Poe chose deliberately.</li>
      <li>The raven: a talking bird that enters the narrator's chamber and answers all questions with "Nevermore".</li>
      <li>Theme: grief, loss, the irrational mind's self-destruction under emotional pressure.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Opening Stanza (Public Domain)</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The poem is in the public domain. The opening stanza:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <blockquote class="lore-quote">
      <p>Once upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary,<br/>
      Over many a quaint and curious volume of forgotten lore —<br/>
      While I nodded, nearly napping, suddenly there came a tapping,<br/>
      As of some one gently rapping, rapping at my chamber door.<br/>
      "'Tis some visitor," I muttered, "tapping at my chamber door —<br/>
      Only this and nothing more."</p>
      <footer>— Edgar Allan Poe, "The Raven", 1845. First stanza (public domain).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></footer>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Poe's Own Explanation — The Philosophy of Composition</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In "The Philosophy of Composition" (1846), Poe gave a detailed account of his choices. Key points (paraphrase):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>He chose a raven because it is "a bird of ill omen" and therefore thematically appropriate for a poem about loss and despair.</li>
      <li>He chose "Nevermore" as the refrain because of its sonority — the long "o" and "r" sounds — as well as its meaning. He wanted a single melancholy word that a bird could plausibly repeat.</li>
      <li>He explicitly described his narrator as a man who "applies to the bird the torture of his grief" — the raven does not think or intend; the narrator projects meaning onto its mechanical repetition.</li>
      <li>He claimed to have worked out the poem's structure logically from its effect backward — a claim most scholars treat as a partly ironic literary essay rather than a literal account of composition.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Dickens' Raven and Poe</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In 1841, Poe reviewed Charles Dickens' novel <em>Barnaby Rudge</em> for <em>Graham's Magazine</em>. Dickens' character Barnaby had a pet raven, "Grip", who spoke scattered words and phrases. Poe's review noted that Grip was underused — the raven should have been a more active symbolic presence in the plot. Poe scholars from T.O. Mabbott onward have widely (but not unanimously) inferred that Poe's encounter with Grip influenced "The Raven".<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <p>Dickens himself kept pet ravens in real life; one died and was stuffed, eventually sold and acquired by the Free Library of Philadelphia, where it is still displayed.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>What The Raven Is Not</h2>
    <p>The raven in Poe's poem is a fictional device — an emblem of grief, not a real bird's behaviour. The poem does not reflect actual raven behaviour or biology. It is a work of literary fiction that uses raven symbolism, not a documentation of folk belief or natural history.</p>
    <p>Many websites present Poe's raven as evidence that ravens "symbolise death or bad luck across cultures". This conflates one poet's artistic choice with a universal cultural tradition — a conflation Poe himself would likely have found reductive.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section lore-bridge">
    <h2>The Bird Behind the Story</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Real ravens do not speak words in the wild, but captive ravens can mimic human speech (see: <a href="/questions/can-ravens-talk/">Can ravens talk?</a>). The raven's intelligence, its black plumage, and its carrion-feeding behaviour would have made it a natural symbol of grief for a 19th-century American readership familiar with the bird from wilderness and farmland.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
    <p><a href="/species/common-raven/" class="lore-species-link">Common Raven — species profile →</a></p>
  </section>

